From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375: wire up thermal throttling

sm6375.dtsi describes 26 thermal zones, each with two passive trip points at
90 and 95 degrees and a critical one at 110, but none of them has a
cooling-maps node. So the passive trips do nothing: nothing is throttled on
the way up and the first thing that actually happens is the emergency
shutdown at 110 degrees.

Add the maps. Every CPU already exposes #cooling-cells, and cpufreq cooling
acts per policy, so each zone lists the whole cluster it belongs to: cpu0 to
cpu5 for the little one (they share cpu0_opp_table) and cpu6 and cpu7 for the
big one. cluster0-thermal and cluster1-thermal get the matching cluster.

The trip temperatures are left alone; this only makes the existing ones
effective.

The GPU zones are handled in the board file, since the GPU node is not part
of the SoC dtsi.

